<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-treaty-of-versailles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Option B opens with the exam question this lesson exists for: <strong>Was the Treaty of
    Versailles fair?</strong> Its focus points give you the structure: what did Wilson,
    Clemenceau and Lloyd George each want, why did none of them get everything, what did the
    treaty do to Germany by the end of 1923, and could it be justified at the time? Keep one
    thought in view throughout: <strong>the treaty’s greatest cost was not in gold marks but
    in legitimacy</strong> — the Germans who had to live under it were never allowed to agree
    with it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Big Three: three mandates, one compromise" widget="equationStep" config={bigThreeCfg} />

  <h2>What the treaty actually said</h2>
  <p>
    Everything rested on <strong>Article 231</strong>, the “war guilt clause”, which declared
    Germany and its allies responsible for the war and all its loss and damage — not because
    historians settled the question in 1919, but because legal guilt was the only way to make
    reparations legally owed. On that hinge hung the reparations bill (132 billion gold marks
    as fixed in 1921), and the disarmament clauses: an army capped at 100,000 men, no
    conscription, no air force, no submarines, six battleships, and the Rhineland permanently
    demilitarised so France would always have a head start. Territory went in every direction:
    Alsace-Lorraine to France, Eupen-Malmedy to Belgium, North Schleswig to Denmark after a
    plebiscite, West Prussia and Posen to the new Poland (the Polish Corridor, with Danzig a
    free city), the Saar run by the League for fifteen years, and every colony converted into
    a League mandate administered by a victor. Why did the victors not get everything? Because
    their own publics disagreed with each other and with each treaty-maker: Wilson’s Senate
    would later reject the whole settlement, Clemenceau failed to detach the Rhineland against
    British and American wishes, and Lloyd George blocked a peace so harsh that Germany could
    neither recover nor resist Bolshevism. Versailles was less a designed peace than the
    residue of three incompatible victories.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from armistice to Ruhr: how the treaty landed on Germany, 1918–24" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>Germany under the treaty, 1919–23</h2>
  <p>
    Germans called it a <strong>Diktat</strong>: no German government had been allowed to
    negotiate, and the delegates signed only under threat of renewed invasion. Because the
    army had not been beaten on German soil, the officers’ myth of the
    <em>Dolchstoß</em> — the “stab in the back” by civilians and Socialists at home — took
    root, and the politicians who signed became “the November criminals”. The treaty thus
    poisoned the Republic at birth: every crisis of the next four years could be blamed on
    Versailles. The Kapp Putsch (1920) showed the right was willing to overthrow the state
    rather than disarm. The crisis of 1923 followed from the treaty’s economics: when Germany
    defaulted on coal deliveries, France occupied the Ruhr; passive resistance halted the
    region’s industry, and the government printed money to pay the idled workforce — which
    destroyed the currency. Hyperinflation wiped out savings and pensions, and with them the
    middle classes’ loyalty to the Republic.
  </p>

  <h2>Could it be justified at the time?</h2>
  <p>
    This is where the best essays earn their marks, because the syllabus asks for
    <em>contemporary opinions</em>, not a modern verdict. On the prosecuting side: the treaty
    was markedly milder than what Germany itself had imposed on Russia at Brest-Litovsk
    (March 1918) — roughly a third of Russia’s population, most of its coal and iron taken;
    northern France really did lie in ruins, with its mines flooded and a million-plus of its
    men dead; and a defeated power that had broken Belgian neutrality in 1914 had little
    standing to complain of harsh terms. On the defending side: Keynes predicted that
    reparations at this scale would ruin Europe’s economy together, and he was close to right
    about the 1920s even where he exaggerated the 1930s; Marshal Foch’s “armistice for twenty
    years” proved exactly prophetic; and in Germany the treaty’s terms, however justified,
    arrived through a process that made them universally resented rather than grudgingly
    accepted. A peace could be defensible in content and still fatal in form — Versailles
    managed both problems at once, and the generation that signed it in the Hall of Mirrors
    left the next one holding the bill.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Core Option B key question 1; Keynes, The Economic Consequences of the Peace (1919); MacMillan, Paris 1919.</p>
